  "summary": "Architecture that reinterprets traditional local building techniques and forms using contemporary materials and methods.",
  "description": "Characterized by simplified versions of traditional forms, local materials reimagined with modern techniques, and references to regional architectural heritage without literal replication. It developed as a reaction to globalized modernism, seeking to maintain cultural identity and place-specific architecture in an increasingly homogenized built environment.",
  "culturalContext": "Vernacular Revival — C.F.A. Voysey's English cottage school through most 20th-century nationalisms — reinterpreted local building (thatch, stone, half-timber, veranda) with modern comfort, becoming the default \"tasteful traditional\" architecture for suburbs, resorts and government housing. In Britain it anchored Arts-and-Crafts Domestic Revival; in Scandinavia and the alps it produced national-romantic housing; its grammar (steep roofs, small panes, honest materials) remains the marketing image of \"countryside\" living worldwide.",
